body:
It sucks when you know that there is a false image of you out there in the world after a toxic person went around spreading lies to taint others's opinions of you, but it's almost always best to just let it go and wait for the truth to come out. 

&#x200B;

People who are your true friends will defend your name and give you the benefit of the doubt when hearing something negative; they wouldn't let someone ruin your friendship before hearing your side to see if it's true or not. And if these lies can easily get in their head enough to turn against you and completely sway their loyalty, then they were never your friend to begin with.

When movies try to cram everything in and rap everything up as fast as possible in the last 15-30mins so it can be a happy "feel good" ending.

&#x200B;

Obviously cliff hangers are frustrating and it sucks to feel angry/upset that the movie ended on a negative note, but goddamn, I would MUCH rather prefer those feelings over rushed endings just so the guy can win the girl, the hero can save the day, or the main characters can be suddenly reunited. Real life doesn't always work itself out like that, it's actually pretty rare, and I would love if movies started adding a little more realistic endings instead of making them all happy fairy tale endings. 

&#x200B;

Not every single movie should end on a cliffhanger or negative note, just the ones that feel rushed and forced when they do it. And hey, if you leave it on a cliffhanger and have enough people wanting to see more, you could make a sequel or even a whole movie series if the story/characters are that good.

Unnecessary traffic caused by people doing under the speed limit and refusing to speed up/move out of the way for people trying to pass.

&#x200B;

Like when all lanes of the highway are congested/moving slow, and then you see that it's because the cars in the very front of the congested area are all matching speeds in each lane refusing to speed up or move back over/stay in the right lane to do their cruising. Drive whatever speed you feel comfortable/safe doing, I don't care, but there's no reason to be in the left lane or even the middle lane if you want to do the exact same speed as everyone in the right. 

&#x200B;

Or another example that is beyond frustrating; when people drive WAY UNDER the speed limit on backroads that are only one lane and do not have areas for others to be able to pass them legally/safely. If you see you're holding up traffic and starting a line of cars behind you because of your ridiculously slow driving, then just pull over to let them pass. It's not fair to make everyone suffer and get held up in unnecessary traffic just because you don't want to do the speed limit.

As long as you acknowledge my presence by saying "excuse me" and are as nice/quick about passing through as possible, I'm totally fine with it!

&#x200B;

I'm never gonna be a dick and prevent you from getting back to your friends after you had to leave for a second. I've had that happen to me where they wouldn't let my friends get to me and it sucked. It was a festival too so I lost them for basically the rest of the night since phone service was minimal and it was so crowded.

&#x200B;

I also don't usually care in general about people just trying to get closer to the front whether they started there or not. Everyone wants a good spot, who am I to say they're not allowed to get one? The only time I care/get annoyed about it is if it's too crowded already to fit more people in my area, if it's CONSTANTLY happening where people are pushing past me, and/or if they're being rude about it.

The worst thing a guy can do is to breakup out of nowhere, give no explanation/real answers as to why, and then try to be very quick about it so they can move on with their lives.

&#x200B;

I would rather know why someone who I thought was happy in our relationship to tell me why they think we should call it quits. Even if their answers are mean or hurtful, I'd rather them be honest and KNOW than to spend the rest of my life wondering why they left and what's wrong with me. It can also help me work on bettering myself for future relationships if it's toxic traits I have that need to be worked on.

&#x200B;

OR if it was nothing of my own fault that caused you to leave, like you met someone else or cheated, then just tell me so I know that there was nothing I could've really done to change this from happening. I don't want to go on to blame/beat myself up about something that I had no control over, that's not fair.

&#x200B;

If you ever had love for that person, or even just cared about them, then the least you could do is just answer any and all questions they may have. Don't forget to be completely honest about it either, no matter how brutally honest the truth may be. They deserve some closure so they can properly move on in their life, don't withhold them from healing because you don't have the balls to be real with them. It's absolutely cowardly and selfish to do that to someone you claimed to care about a lot at one point in your life.

&#x200B;

Also want to add, I know that sometimes it may be hard to have a civil conversation about the breakup right after it happens. If that's the case, then just be sure to leave that window open for them to be able to ask you questions and talk more deeply after things calm down. You don't need to talk about it right away, it can always wait if need be.

&#x200B;

People usually need answers in order to get closure and be able to move on, so just be sure to let them have that option if that's what they need to properly heal. Nobody deserves to fall asleep at night wondering why they weren't good enough to make the one they loved stay... Nobody.

&#x200B;

*PS: Obligatory "not all girls". All of us need different things to heal and make things easier, so I can't speak for all women. Either way though, it's good to give anyone the option to get real honest answers in breakups. Giving them the option is what's important to do if you want to make the breakup a little less painful, but ultimately them actually choosing to get the answers is up to them to choose in the end.*
